<BaseLayout
  title="How insurance discounts work"
  description="Common car and home insurance discount categories, and why availability varies by insurer and state."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>How insurance discounts work</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Insurers offer discounts to attract and keep the customers they want.
      The categories below are common across the industry, but
      <strong>discount availability varies by insurer and state</strong> —
      don't assume a discount is available just because it exists somewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Auto insurance discount categories</h2>

    <h3>Driver discounts</h3>
    <p>
      Often tied to a clean driving record, completing a defensive-driving or
      accident-prevention course, being a student with good grades, or (in
      some states) completing a mature-driver course.
    </p>

    <h3>Vehicle discounts</h3>
    <p>
      Anti-theft devices, safety equipment, and driver-assistance technology
      can qualify a vehicle for a discount with some insurers.
    </p>

    <h3>Policy discounts</h3>
    <p>
      Bundling multiple policies (auto and home, for example), insuring
      multiple vehicles, paying automatically or in full, and choosing
      paperless billing are common ways to reduce price.
    </p>

    <h3>Usage-based and telematics programs</h3>
    <p>
      Some insurers offer a program that tracks driving behavior — speed,
      braking, mileage, time of day — through an app or device, and adjusts
      price based on what it observes. These programs can lower your premium,
      but some can also raise it if the data shows higher-risk driving, so
      it's worth asking how a specific program handles that before enrolling.
    </p>

    <h2>Home insurance discount categories</h2>
    <p>
      Home insurers commonly consider security systems, fire alarms and
      sprinklers, a newer roof, and updated electrical or plumbing systems.
      Bundling with an auto policy is common here too.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        The only way to know which discounts actually apply to you is to ask
        your insurer directly, and to check your declarations page for what's
        already been applied. Where we've verified a specific state's
        discount rules from a primary source, they're shown on that state's
        page — see <a href="/auto-insurance/">auto</a> and
        <a href="/home-insurance/">home</a> by state.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
